# Peka — Co-op 2D Platform Puzzler

> Game Design Document (living draft). Filled out as we ideate.
>
> **Decided:** symmetric players + co-op verbs · local couch · mysterious/eerie tone · lightless caverns · **mirrored-gravity core (flip-at-will · shared tunnel · fall = death)**

---

## 1. Description

A **2D co-op platform game** built around **cooperative puzzles**. Two players
travel together through **one long level** in a system of **lightless caverns**.
Each player can **flip their own gravity at will** — sticking to the floor or the
ceiling of a **shared tunnel**, with a lethal gap between. The level is designed
so it is **impossible to complete solo**: clearing it requires two bodies
oriented and positioned in ways one player can never achieve alone.

Tone is **mysterious and eerie** — sparse, atmospheric, told through the
environment rather than text (Limbo / Inside lineage). Two small, vulnerable
figures; a large, unexplained dark.

**Core pillars**
- **Forced cooperation** — no section clears without two players.
- **Flip-at-will gravity** — the signature verb; floor/ceiling is fluid (see §2).
- **Puzzle-first platforming** — movement is the verb; the brain-teaser is the challenge.
- **One handcrafted journey** — a single, long, escalating level.
- **Wordless atmosphere** — eerie mood, environmental storytelling, minimal UI.

---

## 2. Cooperation Model — ✅ Symmetric + ✅ Mirrored Gravity

Both players control **identical** characters. Either can **flip their own
gravity at any time**, falling toward floor or ceiling of one continuous
**shared tunnel**. Falling into the central gap (no surface to catch you, or onto
a hazard) = **death → shared checkpoint**.

### ⭐ Core mechanic — ✅ decided
- ✅ **Flip at will** — press to invert your own gravity, anytime.
- ✅ **Shared tunnel** — one continuous space; floor and ceiling each have real geometry.
- ✅ **Fall = death** — flipping mid-fall redirects you toward the other surface;
      dying happens when neither surface catches you / you hit a hazard.
- [ ] Confirm: does flipping mid-air **redirect a fall** (a traversal tool), or only work while grounded?

### ⚠️ Central design tension — how we *force* cooperation
Because each player can be on either surface whenever they like, a careless
puzzle is **soloable**. Co-op is enforced by designing around what **one body
cannot do**:
- **Simultaneity** — two switches/weights held *at once*, far apart and on opposite surfaces; one player can occupy only one place/orientation at a time.
- **Bodies as platforms** — flip to the ceiling and stand to become a foothold or hazard-block so your partner can cross the lethal gap.
- **Asymmetric reach** — your lever clears your partner's hazard / builds their landing; neither can reach their own.
This discipline is the heart of level design — I'll solve for it in every puzzle.

### Cooperative verbs — _draft_
- **Hold-two-at-once** — simultaneous opposite-surface switches/plates.
- **Be the landing** — partner becomes the platform/ledge across the gap.
- **Build the crossing** — one moves a block / extends a ledge so the other can flip-jump across.
- **Clear the path** — your input removes the other's hazard or opens their gate.
- **Coordinated flip** — flip together to move a shared counterweight / object.
- [ ] Lock the final ~4–6

